Now I have the full test file. Let me perform the formal 19-smell audit with integration-test calibration.

---

## testsmells.org 19-Smell Audit — `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests`

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 / .NET 10 · **Scope:** 1 file, 8 test methods
**Integration marker detected:** `[TestCategory("Integration")]` — calibration rules applied.

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 2           | 2              |
| Medium   | 2           | 2              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4 of 8 (50%)** |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### ❌ HIGH 1 — Conditional Test Logic *(Smell #1)*

**Test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 95)
**Rationale:** An `if/else` branch changes which assertion runs. If `loaded` is never a `PremiumUser`, the `DiscountRate` branch is dead code — a hidden testing gap.

```csharp
// SMELL — different assertions in each branch
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Fix:** Split into two deterministic tests, each with explicit setup:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("vip@example.com", "Vera", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("vip@example.com")!;

    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** One branch may never execute, giving false confidence that both paths are covered.

---

#### ❌ HIGH 2 — Assertion-Free Test / Unknown Test *(Smell #4)*

**Test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)
**Rationale:** The method exercises 100 inserts but has **zero assertions**. MSTest will report it as passing even if every insert silently fails or corrupts data.

```csharp
// SMELL — no assertion, passes unconditionally unless an exception is thrown
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
}
```

**Fix:** Assert on the observable outcome:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    const int count = 100;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(count, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** 100% false-pass rate — this test can never fail except via unhandled exception.

---

#### ⚠️ MEDIUM 3 — Sleepy Test *(Smell #3)* — downgraded from High per integration calibration

**Test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82)
**Rationale:** `Thread.Sleep(3000)` introduces a hard 3-second wall-clock wait. Even in an integration suite, this is non-deterministic (may be too short under CI load, always too long when the event fires instantly) and slows the suite.

```csharp
// SMELL — blocking sleep, also uses Thread.Sleep in an async method
Thread.Sleep(3000);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Fix:** Poll with a timeout instead of a fixed sleep:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
    var poll = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100);
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow + timeout;

    while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
    {
        if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
            break;
        await Task.Delay(poll);
    }

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        "Notification was not sent within the timeout period.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky failures in CI (race condition); adds 3 s to every run even when the event fires in milliseconds.

---

#### ⚠️ MEDIUM 4 — Assertion Roulette *(Extended Smell)*

**Test:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28)
**Rationale:** 4 assertion calls with **no message parameters**. When one fails, the developer sees only a line number and a generic "Expected: X, Actual: Y" — no explanation of *which property* was wrong.

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);   // what does this check?
```

**Fix:** Add descriptive messages:

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should persist.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should persist.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower triage — developers must read the test source to understand which property failed and why.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

These 4 tests are well-written and follow integration-test best practices:

| Test | Why it's clean |
|------|----------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert, focused assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single precise assertion (`Assert.IsNull`) |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | `3` is contextually obvious (3 items just inserted); `CollectionAssert` is appropriate |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Properly awaited, count matches setup, good concurrency test |

No instances of Mystery Guest (SQLite in-memory is hermetic), Sensitive Equality, Exception Handling, Magic Number, General Fixture, Eager Test, Duplicate Assert, Ignored Test, Redundant Print, Redundant Assertion, Default Test, Constructor Initialization, Empty Test, Resource Optimism, or Lazy Test were found.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | **Assertion-Free Test** — `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` | Add `Assert.AreEqual(count, users.Count)` | ~2 min |
| 2 | **Conditional Test Logic** — `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` | Split into two deterministic tests | ~10 min |
| 3 | **Sleepy Test** — `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with polling loop | ~10 min |
| 4 | **Assertion Roulette** — `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` | Add message strings to each assertion | ~2 min |

**Bottom line:** Half the suite (4/8) carries a smell. The Assertion-Free Test is the most dangerous — it literally cannot fail. Fix that first, then eliminate the Conditional Test Logic to close the hidden coverage gap.
